title: The forest in the store
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: Each tree_ref node keeps the bitmap of its descendants and of its children, so a subtree is one read and a move costs the depth, not the size.

For every `tree_ref` field, the store keeps two bitmaps per node
([[Store tables]], set kinds ''descendants'' and ''referrers''):

* the ids of ''all'' its descendants;
* the ids pointing at it — a folder's children, a `ref`'s referrers.

!! Keeping them up to date

A node holds one position per forest ([[One position per forest]]), so maintaining
them is set arithmetic along the ancestor chain:

* ''a node is created'' under a parent: its id joins each ancestor's descendants — about depth chunk writes;
* ''a subtree moves'': its ids leave each old ancestor's descendants and join each new one's — about depth operations, ''independent of the subtree's size'' (a move of 750 000 records took 6 ms, measured);
* ''a rename'' changes no bitmap: only the node's `forest` key moves.

The exception is a `hierarchy_ref` node of several parents: when it loses one, its
descendants are recomputed from its ancestors' children ([[Hierarchy references]]).

The price is about depth times more chunks per tree write. Opening a folder — the
GUI's central gesture — costs a handful of reads in return.

!! What it buys

* ''A subtree'' — `field ->* "/dir"` ([[Following references]]) — is one bitmap read; so is a tag subtree (`tag ->* (path =>* "music")`).
* ''A folder'' is its children bitmap: a key per 65 536 children.
* ''The forest invariants'' (no cycle, depth ≤ 1000) need only the ancestor chain: about depth reads per checked write.

!! Sorting by path

Below a bound (a folder, a subtree), a path sort walks the forest in key order — each
folder's children read a page at a time, ordered by name bytes — and stops at the
page's end; the next page resumes by descending its cursor key's components. It
declines, and the page is fetched and sorted instead ([[Sorting and postings]]):

* without a bound;
* on a name that is not plain UTF-8 above the separator;
* on a name keyed by hash (over 300 bytes).

!! Order-sensitive `osm` paths

An `osm` path ([[Text search in the store]]) finds its candidate ''anchors'' by
trigrams, verifies each on its assembled path (ancestors read once, shared between
candidates), and takes the union of the verified anchors' subtrees. When an earlier
term's rarest trigram names fewer folders than half the anchors, the anchors are
first cut to those folders' subtrees (`dir4321 file` on 100 000 records: 210 008 keys
read → 75).

!! `:path` comparisons

A `:path` comparison seeks instead of walking, wherever the pattern allows:

* `field:path = "<p>"` goes down `p`'s names, one read per level;
* a pattern anchored on a literal prefix (`^/music/j`) seeks the folder of the prefix's last separator;
* an ordering (`<`, `>`…) enters only the subtrees that can fall on the right side;
* a pattern anchored at the end on a literal (`\.mp3$`, `jazz/a\.mp3$`) reads, by trigrams, the names that literal's end lies in, each verified on its assembled path (`000123\.txt$` on 16 000 records: 32 326 keys read → 19); when the pattern also has a prefix reaching below a forest's root, the prefix is used, being narrower still;
* a pattern anchored nowhere on a literal (`mp3`, `(?i)MP3$`) still walks the whole forest.

!! Tests

`tests/kv_cost.rs` pins it: a page of a 16 000-entry folder sorted by path, a subtree
by path, and a count over a subtree of 800 folders read what they read on an eighth
of it.
